.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/resolve-from Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "resolve-from [![Build Status](https://travis-ci.org/sindresorhus/resolve-from.svg?branch=master)](https://travis-ci.org/sindresorhus/resolve-from)"
.PP
.RS 4
Resolve the path of a module like \fR\fRrequire\&.resolve()\fP\fP but from a given path 
.RE
.PP
.SH "Install"
.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install resolve\-from
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
const resolveFrom = require('resolve\-from');

// There is a file at `\&./foo/bar\&.js`

resolveFrom('foo', '\&./bar');
//=> '/Users/sindresorhus/dev/test/foo/bar\&.js'
.fi
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "resolveFrom(fromDir, moduleId)"
Like \fRrequire()\fP, throws when the module can't be found\&.
.SS "resolveFrom\&.silent(fromDir, moduleId)"
Returns \fRnull\fP instead of throwing when the module can't be found\&.
.SS "fromDir"
Type: \fRstring\fP

.PP
Directory to resolve from\&.
.SS "moduleId"
Type: \fRstring\fP

.PP
What you would use in \fRrequire()\fP\&.
.SH "Tip"
.PP
Create a partial using a bound function if you want to resolve from the same \fRfromDir\fP multiple times:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const resolveFromFoo = resolveFrom\&.bind(null, 'foo');

resolveFromFoo('\&./bar');
resolveFromFoo('\&./baz');
.fi
.PP
